1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,010
Computational Skepticism for AI. Probability and the Confidence Illusion. Learn why

2
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:09,505
a confident number is not a correct number, and the four questions that check it

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:10,970
before you trust it.

4
00:00:11,790 --> 00:00:16,613
Hola. This is Liam, in for Bear, with a lecture for INFO 7375, Computational

5
00:00:16,633 --> 00:00:22,148
Skepticism for AI. A model hands you a score of point nine four, and the answer

6
00:00:22,168 --> 00:00:24,570
looks settled. It is not settled yet.

7
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:29,864
Six terms. An event is a set of outcomes you care about. A classifier is a model

8
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:34,535
that sorts cases into classes. A confidence score is the number it attaches to its

9
00:00:34,555 --> 00:00:38,895
answer. Held-out data is cases kept back from training. A distribution is how often

10
00:00:38,915 --> 00:00:42,320
each value occurs. And variance is how spread out values are.

11
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:44,710
A Number That Looks Like a Fact.

12
00:00:44,730 --> 00:00:49,369
A classifier scores a batch of records and hands back a column of probabilities.

13
00:00:49,389 --> 00:00:54,029
Point nine four. Point nine one. Point eight eight. Sorted, clean, ready for a

14
00:00:54,049 --> 00:00:55,360
dashboard. It looks finished.

15
00:00:55,380 --> 00:01:00,424
But that column is degrees of belief the model holds, formatted to look like facts

16
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:05,150
about the world. Nothing in it says whether ninety-four of every hundred such cases

17
00:01:05,170 --> 00:01:06,500
really turn out positive.

18
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:11,658
It does not say how common the thing you are hunting is. And it does not say whether

19
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:15,670
training rewarded the model for announcing point nine four when it should have meant

20
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:16,530
point eight five.

21
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,877
That gap, between the confidence a system radiates and the confidence it has earned,

22
00:01:20,897 --> 00:01:25,225
is the subject. The habit that closes it is plausibility auditing: hearing the wrong

23
00:01:25,245 --> 00:01:28,330
note in a number before you can prove it wrong.

24
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:32,723
Three tools do the work. A calculation that repairs your intuition about rare

25
00:01:32,743 --> 00:01:37,453
events. A diagnostic that tells you whether a model's numbers mean what they say.

26
00:01:37,473 --> 00:01:41,170
And a warning about the one situation where averages stop working.

27
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:43,880
Probability Is a Degree of Belief.

28
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:49,629
Most engineers come up Boolean. A bit is one or zero. A test passes or fails. Even

29
00:01:49,649 --> 00:01:54,364
error bars arrive as a small wobble around a yes-or-no answer. Probability is a

30
00:01:54,384 --> 00:01:55,040
different animal.

31
00:01:55,060 --> 00:02:00,616
Its output is not true or false. It is a degree of belief: a number from zero to one

32
00:02:00,636 --> 00:02:03,550
saying how confident to be, given what you currently know.

33
00:02:03,570 --> 00:02:08,471
So two engineers can look at the same evidence, land on different probabilities, and

34
00:02:08,491 --> 00:02:12,337
both reason correctly. They walked in with different priors, different starting

35
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:15,500
beliefs. Probability is always conditional on what you know.

36
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,850
To compute with it you need three objects. A sample space: every possible outcome,

37
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:25,133
for a die, one through six. An event: any subset, like the even faces two, four and

38
00:02:25,153 --> 00:02:28,240
six. And a measure that assigns each event a number.

39
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:33,144
That measure must obey three axioms. Never negative. The whole sample space has

40
00:02:33,164 --> 00:02:38,048
probability one, because something has to happen. And for events that cannot both

41
00:02:38,068 --> 00:02:39,180
occur, probabilities add.

42
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:43,143
Two consequences follow at once. Nothing happening has probability zero. And the

43
00:02:43,163 --> 00:02:48,098
chance that an event does not happen is one minus the chance that it does.

44
00:02:48,118 --> 00:02:50,740
Everything else here is built from these rules.

45
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:54,892
There are two natural ways to assign the numbers. Classical, when outcomes are

46
00:02:54,912 --> 00:03:00,001
equally likely: a bag holds one ball each of five colors, so drawing yellow is one

47
00:03:00,021 --> 00:03:00,640
in five.

48
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:05,373
Frequentist, for repeatable experiments: toss a coin twenty times and seven heads is

49
00:03:05,393 --> 00:03:10,470
a frequency of seven twentieths. Keep tossing, and if the process is stable, that

50
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:12,290
frequency settles toward a limit.

51
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:16,432
Neither answers what a deployed system faces every minute: given this output, right

52
00:03:16,452 --> 00:03:20,575
now, how should my belief about this case change? That is Bayesian probability.

53
00:03:20,595 --> 00:03:25,673
Start with a prior, see evidence, update to a posterior. Use all three, and do not

54
00:03:25,693 --> 00:03:26,310
get tribal.

55
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:28,530
Conditioning and the Chain.

56
00:03:28,550 --> 00:03:32,825
Conditioning is the engine. Learning that B happened shrinks the world to B. The

57
00:03:32,845 --> 00:03:38,346
probability of A given B is the part of B that also lies in A: the overlap, divided

58
00:03:38,366 --> 00:03:38,960
by B.

59
00:03:38,980 --> 00:03:44,355
A worked case. In a log of a triage model's cases, seventy-six percent arrive with

60
00:03:44,375 --> 00:03:48,671
an abnormal lab flag, forty-five percent turn out high-acuity, and thirty percent

61
00:03:48,691 --> 00:03:49,390
are both.

62
00:03:49,410 --> 00:03:53,482
A flagged case arrives. Thirty over seventy-six: about thirty-nine point five

63
00:03:53,502 --> 00:03:58,690
percent. Lower than the forty-five percent you would guess with no flag at all.

64
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:02,677
Why lower? If flag and acuity were unrelated, both would show up together

65
00:04:02,697 --> 00:04:07,277
thirty-four point two percent of the time. They show up thirty percent. In this log,

66
00:04:07,297 --> 00:04:11,877
the flag goes with high acuity less than chance. The formula does the work intuition

67
00:04:11,897 --> 00:04:12,490
will not.

68
00:04:12,510 --> 00:04:16,488
Rearrange the definition and you get the multiplication rule: the chance that both

69
00:04:16,508 --> 00:04:21,409
happen is the chance of the first, times the chance of the second given the first.

70
00:04:21,429 --> 00:04:25,100
Chain it, and you can reason about anything that happens in sequence.

71
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:30,327
An agent must land three tool calls in order. The first succeeds nine times in ten.

72
00:04:30,347 --> 00:04:34,573
Given that, the second succeeds eighty-five percent of the time; given both, the

73
00:04:34,593 --> 00:04:37,840
third, eighty. Multiply along the path: point six one two.

74
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:43,222
So the agent's one word, task complete, is one bit. What it claims is a product of

75
00:04:43,242 --> 00:04:46,704
three shrinking conditionals: about sixty-one percent. The report is shorter than

76
00:04:46,724 --> 00:04:47,970
the thing it reports.

77
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:53,438
The tempting shortcut treats the steps as independent, nine in ten each. Nine tenths

78
00:04:53,458 --> 00:04:57,735
cubed is seventy-three percent: nearly twelve points of confidence nobody earned.

79
00:04:57,755 --> 00:05:00,860
Real failures cluster, so this error runs optimistic.

80
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:05,213
The total probability theorem decomposes a rate you cannot measure directly. Split

81
00:05:05,233 --> 00:05:09,929
the world into scenarios that do not overlap and cover everything, find the

82
00:05:09,949 --> 00:05:13,920
probability inside each, weight by how often each occurs, and add.

83
00:05:13,940 --> 00:05:18,035
A fraud-screening pipeline runs three model versions. A handles forty percent of

84
00:05:18,055 --> 00:05:23,179
cases and errs two percent, B thirty and three, C thirty and five. Overall error:

85
00:05:23,199 --> 00:05:27,980
point oh three two, three point two percent, a number no single version reports.

86
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,280
The Ninety-Nine Percent Test.

87
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:35,581
Now a test for a disease that about one person in ten thousand has. The test is

88
00:05:35,601 --> 00:05:39,322
ninety-nine percent accurate. A patient tests positive. Before reading on, pick a

89
00:05:39,342 --> 00:05:42,440
number: how likely is it that this patient is sick?

90
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:47,546
Most people say above ninety percent. In a classic 1978 study, Casscells,

91
00:05:47,566 --> 00:05:52,227
Schoenberger and Graboys asked sixty Harvard physicians and students a harder

92
00:05:52,247 --> 00:05:57,333
version: one in a thousand, five percent false positives. Eleven answered correctly.

93
00:05:57,353 --> 00:05:58,610
Twenty-seven said ninety-five.

94
00:05:58,630 --> 00:06:03,871
Count bodies instead. Ten thousand people take the test. On average one is sick, and

95
00:06:03,891 --> 00:06:08,080
the test catches that one. Nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine are healthy.

96
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:12,972
Ninety-nine percent accurate also means one percent of healthy people test positive.

97
00:06:12,992 --> 00:06:18,272
One percent of nine thousand nine hundred ninety-nine is about a hundred false

98
00:06:18,292 --> 00:06:18,680
positives.

99
00:06:18,700 --> 00:06:24,116
So a hundred and one people test positive, and one of them is sick. One in a hundred

100
00:06:24,136 --> 00:06:26,230
and one. About one percent, not ninety-nine.

101
00:06:26,250 --> 00:06:30,211
You answered the right question in the wrong direction. Ninety-nine percent is the

102
00:06:30,231 --> 00:06:35,111
chance of a positive given the disease. You needed the chance of the disease given a

103
00:06:35,131 --> 00:06:38,480
positive. Getting from one to the other takes the base rate.

104
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,550
Bayes Runs Backward.

105
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:45,494
That conversion is Bayes' theorem. The posterior is the prior times the likelihood,

106
00:06:45,514 --> 00:06:50,059
divided by everything that could produce a positive: the total probability theorem,

107
00:06:50,079 --> 00:06:51,580
run the other way.

108
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:55,768
Run it. Prior one in ten thousand, hit rate point nine nine, false-alarm rate point

109
00:06:55,788 --> 00:07:00,793
oh one. The answer is exactly one in a hundred and two. The simple count said one in

110
00:07:00,813 --> 00:07:04,980
a hundred and one, because it let the test catch the sick person for certain.

111
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,985
The positive result did real work: it raised the probability about ninety-eight

112
00:07:09,005 --> 00:07:13,992
times. But the prior was so low that the posterior is still small. Both facts

113
00:07:14,012 --> 00:07:17,330
matter, and neither shows if you read only the accuracy.

114
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:21,678
One honest caution. Ninety-nine percent accurate was doing double duty, as the rate

115
00:07:21,698 --> 00:07:26,695
for catching the sick and as the rate for clearing the healthy. Those are two

116
00:07:26,715 --> 00:07:30,040
numbers, sensitivity and specificity, and a real test has two.

117
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:34,583
Run Bayes backward on the fleet. A wrong output lands on your desk. Which version

118
00:07:34,603 --> 00:07:39,430
made it? C handles thirty percent of traffic, but point oh one five over point oh

119
00:07:39,450 --> 00:07:43,367
three two means forty-seven percent of the errors. Not A, the busiest, at

120
00:07:43,387 --> 00:07:43,670
twenty-five.

121
00:07:43,690 --> 00:07:45,440
Rare Events Everywhere.

122
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:49,761
Slide the base rate and watch. At ninety-nine and ninety-nine, a positive is a coin

123
00:07:49,781 --> 00:07:54,945
flip at one in a hundred, nine percent at one in a thousand, one percent at one in

124
00:07:54,965 --> 00:07:58,690
ten thousand, a tenth of a percent at one in a hundred thousand.

125
00:07:58,710 --> 00:08:03,319
This structure appears wherever the thing you hunt is rare: fraud, intrusions,

126
00:08:03,339 --> 00:08:07,561
screening, moderation. An excellent detector then raises mostly false alarms. Only

127
00:08:07,581 --> 00:08:12,190
two escapes exist: raise the base rate, or accept more missed cases.

128
00:08:12,210 --> 00:08:16,171
Operators check the first alerts. False. The next few. False. Within weeks they

129
00:08:16,191 --> 00:08:20,764
learn the alarms mean nothing, and they miss the real one. That is not carelessness.

130
00:08:20,784 --> 00:08:23,520
It is a structural fact nobody warned them about.

131
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:28,440
Wong and colleagues tested a hospital sepsis model, JAMA Internal Medicine, 2021.

132
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:33,360
Its alert fired on eighteen percent of hospitalizations, while sepsis was seven

133
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:37,050
percent. Alerts were right twelve percent of the time.

134
00:08:37,070 --> 00:08:42,180
And it missed sixty-seven percent of the sepsis cases: seventeen hundred and nine of

135
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,944
twenty-five hundred and fifty-two. Many alarms, and most of the real cases still

136
00:08:46,964 --> 00:08:47,310
unflagged.

137
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:49,400
Every Number Is a Claim.

138
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:54,436
Now move from Boolean to probabilistic. A function returning forty-two is a fact. A

139
00:08:54,456 --> 00:08:59,113
model returning point nine one is a claim, conditional on its parameters, its

140
00:08:59,133 --> 00:09:02,350
training data, and assumptions about where it is deployed.

141
00:09:02,370 --> 00:09:06,970
Take a triage model that flags a patient high-acuity at point eight seven. That

142
00:09:06,990 --> 00:09:11,260
means: if this patient resembles the training data, if the confidence is calibrated,

143
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:15,880
and if nothing has shifted since training. Remove any condition and the number means

144
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,200
something else, or nothing.

145
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:21,925
In the eighteenth century David Hume asked an awkward question. You have seen the

146
00:09:21,945 --> 00:09:26,650
sun rise every morning of your life. Does that guarantee tomorrow? His answer: no.

147
00:09:26,670 --> 00:09:30,700
Induction works because the world cooperates, not because logic forces it to.

148
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:35,292
Translate it. The training set is the past, deployment is the future, and

149
00:09:35,312 --> 00:09:39,531
distribution shift is the technical name for the world declining to cooperate:

150
00:09:39,551 --> 00:09:42,710
inputs drift, base rates move, causes and effects change.

151
00:09:42,730 --> 00:09:47,212
And the model cannot tell. Its confidence was calculated assuming the world it

152
00:09:47,232 --> 00:09:52,061
learned. Watch the inputs slide away while the confidence readout stays put and the

153
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:55,870
dashboard stays green. Only outcomes, when they arrive, show the damage.

154
00:09:55,890 --> 00:10:01,125
A real case: DeGrave, Janizek and Lee, 2021, examined deep learning systems that

155
00:10:01,145 --> 00:10:05,975
detect COVID-19 on chest X-rays. They leaned on confounding shortcuts instead of

156
00:10:05,995 --> 00:10:11,230
lung pathology, so they looked accurate in testing and failed at new hospitals.

157
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:15,588
The honest question about any track record: what would have to remain true about the

158
00:10:15,608 --> 00:10:19,945
deployment for the past to stay informative? If you cannot answer that, you do not

159
00:10:19,965 --> 00:10:22,560
yet trust the record. You just have not noticed.

160
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:23,750
Calibration.

161
00:10:23,770 --> 00:10:28,414
Calibration. A model is calibrated when its stated probabilities match what happens.

162
00:10:28,434 --> 00:10:33,855
Say seventy percent on a thousand cases, and about seven hundred should turn out

163
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:38,130
positive. If four hundred do, it is overconfident; nine hundred, underconfident.

164
00:10:38,150 --> 00:10:42,723
Put stated confidence across and actual frequency up. A calibrated model hugs the

165
00:10:42,743 --> 00:10:48,377
diagonal. A miscalibrated one peels away, and the shape of the peel says how it is

166
00:10:48,397 --> 00:10:48,730
wrong.

167
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:53,716
Here is a classifier from our own simulation: twenty thousand cases, labels we

168
00:10:53,736 --> 00:10:57,934
generated, seventy-five percent accurate. Sorted by stated confidence, it is near

169
00:10:57,954 --> 00:11:02,536
honest in the middle and badly overconfident at the top: stating ninety-seven

170
00:11:02,556 --> 00:11:04,070
percent, right about eighty-six.

171
00:11:04,090 --> 00:11:09,974
This is not one bad model. Guo, Pleiss, Sun and Weinberger, at ICML 2017, found that

172
00:11:09,994 --> 00:11:14,771
modern neural networks, unlike those of a decade earlier, are poorly calibrated, and

173
00:11:14,791 --> 00:11:18,830
that depth, width, weight decay and batch normalization all affect it.

174
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:23,644
Their cheap fix is temperature scaling. Divide the logits, the scores before the

175
00:11:23,664 --> 00:11:28,829
softmax, by one learned number T, then apply the softmax. With T above one,

176
00:11:28,849 --> 00:11:30,310
confidence softens toward honesty.

177
00:11:30,330 --> 00:11:35,915
We fit T on held-out data: two point one nine. The dots slide onto the diagonal. The

178
00:11:35,935 --> 00:11:40,202
top bin now states ninety-four percent and is right ninety-four. Accuracy did not

179
00:11:40,222 --> 00:11:42,510
change, and neither did a single decision.

180
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:46,615
So confidence and correctness were separable all along. The number was decorative,

181
00:11:46,635 --> 00:11:51,063
and temperature scaling is how you un-decorate it. But it only fixes average

182
00:11:51,083 --> 00:11:53,800
calibration on the data you fit it to.

183
00:11:53,820 --> 00:11:57,659
If the deployment world drifts from that calibration set, the honest number goes

184
00:11:57,679 --> 00:12:02,407
stale, silently: on the tail, on the novel case, on the day the world moves. Honest

185
00:12:02,427 --> 00:12:07,156
confidence bought cheaply on the data you have, paid for in silence on the data you

186
00:12:07,176 --> 00:12:07,750
do not.

187
00:12:07,770 --> 00:12:12,331
So never trust a stated probability without its calibration curve on data from the

188
00:12:12,351 --> 00:12:16,584
actual deployment. And decide in advance what would make you reject the number.

189
00:12:16,604 --> 00:12:20,510
Without that, you are judging a score by how confident it sounds.

190
00:12:20,530 --> 00:12:24,853
Calibration applies to you, too. Ask people for ninety percent intervals, and the

191
00:12:24,873 --> 00:12:29,531
truth falls inside far less often than ninety: reviews of this research put it

192
00:12:29,551 --> 00:12:33,540
between thirty and sixty percent. About half. Run the experiment on yourself.

193
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:35,910
When Averages Stop Settling.

194
00:12:35,930 --> 00:12:40,439
The central limit theorem says sums of many independent variables with finite

195
00:12:40,459 --> 00:12:45,723
variance look like a bell curve, which is why averages are useful. Two requirements

196
00:12:45,743 --> 00:12:49,120
hide inside: independence, and finite variance, a finite spread.

197
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:54,337
Break either, and the failure is quiet. Independence breaks in networks and in time

198
00:12:54,357 --> 00:12:58,064
series that remember. Finite variance breaks in heavy-tailed distributions, where

199
00:12:58,084 --> 00:13:01,790
extreme events are too common for averaging to dampen them.

200
00:13:01,810 --> 00:13:06,546
Watch the running average of five thousand draws. From a bell curve, it settles near

201
00:13:06,566 --> 00:13:10,350
zero. From a Cauchy distribution it never does. One draw, minus forty-three

202
00:13:10,370 --> 00:13:15,423
thousand, moved it by almost twelve, and at five thousand it sat at minus six point

203
00:13:15,443 --> 00:13:15,740
eight.

204
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:20,232
For deployment, the question is the distribution of the cost of being wrong.

205
00:13:20,252 --> 00:13:24,724
Suppose, with made-up numbers, a security alert system wastes ten dollars on each

206
00:13:24,744 --> 00:13:28,180
false alarm, but a missed breach costs five hundred thousand.

207
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:32,949
An average-loss metric will look beautiful and answer the wrong question, because

208
00:13:32,969 --> 00:13:38,116
the rare catastrophic case is the only one that matters. Use tail-aware metrics,

209
00:13:38,136 --> 00:13:42,090
worst-case analysis, and adversarial inputs that surface catastrophes in testing.

210
00:13:42,110 --> 00:13:47,187
The honest admission: there is no clean way to tell, before the catastrophe, that a

211
00:13:47,207 --> 00:13:51,264
loss is heavy-tailed. Estimating tails from a sample is mathematically impolite. The

212
00:13:51,284 --> 00:13:56,020
working rule, a heuristic and not a calculation: assume heavy tails when errors can

213
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:56,700
harm people.

214
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:58,000
Four Questions.

215
00:13:58,020 --> 00:14:02,713
Most failures of confidence are not failures of competence. They are failures of

216
00:14:02,733 --> 00:14:06,702
interpretation: intuition that forgets the prior, trusts the stated number, averages

217
00:14:06,722 --> 00:14:10,690
over a heavy tail, and assumes the world has not changed.

218
00:14:10,710 --> 00:14:15,401
So ask four questions, in order. What is the base rate? Is the model calibrated on

219
00:14:15,421 --> 00:14:19,818
data from this deployment? What is the distribution of the cost of being wrong? And

220
00:14:19,838 --> 00:14:21,290
what has changed since training?

221
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:27,590
One boundary on all of it. A method that reliably detected shift from outputs alone,

222
00:14:27,610 --> 00:14:32,210
without outcomes, would change this argument. Rabanser, Günnemann and Lipton, at

223
00:14:32,230 --> 00:14:35,990
